The PDP-11 emulator on Debian
===============

Run qunilator-setup and you are done:

    sudo qunilator-setup

It settles the boot configuration, checks that the PRUs and the cape are
there, bridges the uplink so the emulated machine can reach the network,
tells the emulated Ethernet controller which interface to bind to, and
starts the services. It prints the address to point a browser at.

Running it again is harmless. If a bridge already carries the uplink it is
kept as it is, rather than rebuilt.

On a kernel newer than 4.19 the first run usually changes /boot/uEnv.txt and
stops there, because those settings only take effect at boot:

    sudo qunilator-setup      # applies boot settings, asks for a reboot
    sudo reboot
    sudo qunilator-setup      # finishes

The original file is kept as /boot/uEnv.txt.bone-orig.

Boot settings
-------------

bone_capemgr was removed in 4.19, so the cape overlay no longer loads from
the cape's own EEPROM and U-Boot applies it instead. qunilator-setup names
QBone.dtbo in a free overlay slot, and turns off three overlays that would
otherwise claim the cape's pins first - the eMMC's, HDMI's and the audio
codec's. It also comments out uboot_overlay_pru: that overlay names a
PRU-ICSS compatible no driver has bound since 6.10, so applying it takes the
PRUs away rather than providing them.

Beyond the cape's pins, QBone.dtbo declares the memory the emulated machine
runs in and the interrupt the PRUs raise. uio_pdrv_genirq binds to the
latter only when told which compatible to look for, which
/etc/modprobe.d/bone.conf does.

If qunilator-setup reports a missing piece it names which one, and each maps to
one of these settings.

qunilator-setup runs in two passes. The first applies the boot settings, builds
the legacy-Ethernet device tree and writes the bridge configuration; it then
asks for a reboot, because both the device tree and the bridge take effect
only at boot. The second, after the reboot, finds everything in place and
starts the services. Run it, reboot when it says to, and run it again.

The distribution image does this without a login: qunilator-setup.service runs
"qunilator-setup --auto", which reboots between the passes itself and records
/var/lib/qunilator/.setup-done when the network is configured, so it runs only
until the board is set up. The unit ships disabled on a package install,
where the operator runs qunilator-setup by hand; the image enables it.

Moving eth0 onto the bridge moves this board's address onto the bridge, and
the device tree swap changes the Ethernet driver -- either could put a
headless board out of reach if something is wrong. There is no live rollback:
the changes are applied by the reboot, so keep a serial console to hand the
first time. The stock device tree is kept as
/boot/dtbs/<kernel>/am335x-boneblack-uboot.dtb.stock; restoring it and
rebooting returns eth0 to its original driver.

To skip the network entirely:

    sudo qunilator-setup --no-network

The emulated machine can then reach this board, which is enough for a local
mopd, but not the rest of the network.

What it sets up
---------------

The emulated Ethernet controller needs a promiscuous interface that carries
the guest's several MAC addresses and reaches both the LAN and this board at
layer 2 (mopd boots the guest over Ethernet). That is a Linux bridge over a
plain NIC. The stock 6.x kernel binds the switchdev cpsw driver, whose eth0
is a hardware switch port the kernel will not add to a software bridge, so
qunilator-setup builds a device tree that binds the legacy ti,cpsw driver and
installs it as the DTB U-Boot loads.

The bridge itself is systemd-networkd: br0 carries eth0 (the uplink) and
veth-br (the controller's veth), and holds this board's DHCP address, pinned
to the uplink's MAC so the lease does not change. qunilator-network creates the
veth pair from /etc/qunilator/network.conf before the emulator starts; the
controller binds to veth-pdp. The networkd files live in
/etc/systemd/network/{br0.netdev,br0.network,eth0.network,veth-br.network,
veth-pdp.network}.

Running
-------

qunilator-setup starts and enables the services. Afterwards they behave as any
others:

    systemctl status qbone      # or unibone, on a UNIBUS board
    systemctl restart qbone

The web interface answers on port 80. State - disk images, saved
configurations and settings.json - lives in /var/lib/qunilator.

The distribution image ships a root filesystem shrunk to fit; qunilator-resize
grows it to fill the card in two stages across a reboot - extend the
partition on the first boot, grow the ext4 into it once the kernel has
re-read the table on the next - then records /var/lib/qunilator/.resized and
does not run again.

/usr/bin/qbone (/usr/bin/unibone on a UNIBUS board) serves the web interface
and has no menu. The machine a board
comes up as is a saved configuration: add "--config <name>" to the unit's
ExecStart to apply one at startup. /usr/bin/qbone-demo is the same emulation
driven from a terminal menu, for bus latch, master/slave and device exerciser
work; it needs an operator on stdin and is not what the service runs.

The emulated Ethernet controller binds to veth-pdp, the interface
qunilator-network creates and joins to the bridge qunilator-setup builds from the
uplink. Rename it in /etc/qunilator/network.conf and the delqa's "interface"
parameter has to follow.

Status LEDs
-----------

qunilator-leds drives a contiguous run of three green user LEDs - usr0, usr1,
usr2 - to show how far the board is through bring-up, and relocates SD-card
activity (the mmc0 trigger, normally usr1) to usr3, whose eMMC trigger is
idle since the board runs from the SD card. A growing blink marks progress
and a bouncing light means the emulator is up:

    X00   booting        blink
    XX0   configuring    blink
    XXX   starting       blink
    bounce               ready

The phase comes from systemd and /var/lib/qunilator/.setup-done, so the bounce
simply means the emulator's service is active. The unit is enabled on the distribution
image and shipped disabled in the package; `systemctl disable --now
qunilator-leds` returns the LEDs to the kernel on the next boot.

Updating
--------

The web interface tells you when a newer package is published, shows what
changed, and installs it. That path is the one to use: it says what an update
costs before it starts, watches the new service come up, and puts the old
version back if it does not.

qunilator-update is the same mechanism from the command line:

    qunilator-update --check       refresh the index, report installed/candidate
    qunilator-update --changelog   the candidate's changelog since installed
    qunilator-update --status      the status file the interface publishes
    qunilator-update --rollback    put the cached previous version back

It reads the apt repository /etc/apt/sources.list.d/<name>.list names, which
the appliance image configures, so apt checks the signed index and the package
hash and there is no unsigned install path. A board installed by hand with
"dpkg -i" has no such source and reports so: `apt install` from the repository
once makes it updateable.

qunilator-update-check.timer runs the check five minutes after boot and daily
after that, with a randomised delay. Nothing is ever installed without someone
asking for it, and /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/51qunilator-unattended keeps
unattended-upgrades away from the emulator package - an unattended upgrade
would stop the emulated machine with no warning.

Installing a new version restarts the service, which stops the emulated
machine. The device set is shut down in order, so the disk images are flushed,
but a running Unix or RSX has its buffer cache cut, and the board comes back up
in its startup configuration. Halt the guest first if that matters.

The updater's state lives in /var/lib/qunilator/updates, root-only: the status
file, the requested version, the staged candidate and a cached copy of the
version the board runs, which is what a rollback reinstalls.

    journalctl -u qunilator-update          an install
    journalctl -u qunilator-update-check    the daily check

The board's other packages are reported by the same check and upgraded with
"qunilator-update --os-upgrade", or from the interface. That path has no
rollback - apt cannot undo an upgrade - so it holds the emulator package back,
upgrades nothing that would remove another package, and never reboots the
board. The kernel stays held from the image build.

Admin password
--------------

A board that has no password answers every request, which is what makes a
freshly installed one reachable. Open the interface and it asks for a
password before anything else; from then on the browser asks for it on every
visit, and any user name is accepted. It is stored in settings.json as a
PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 digest, so that file becomes readable only by root.

Open the interface promptly after installing. Until a password is set, anyone
who can reach port 80 can operate the machine.

Setting WEBUI_PASSWORD in the service's environment overrides all of this:
the password is then whatever the variable says and the interface will not
change it.

The service runs as root: the PRU driver maps physical memory and the bus
engine needs realtime scheduling priority.
